<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> An Environmental Project</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Can We Help the Environment?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Write simple interpretations of common texts in English supported with visual aids. communication: Facilitate a discussion on a group project.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can write a simple interpretation of common texts with visual aids (e.g., write 'recycle paper' and show a recycling symbol). Can facilitate a group project discussion by writing prompts and showing visuals (e.g., 'Who wants to write the title of the project on the board?' and holds up a marker, helping the team decide who will take that role.). Can use visual aids and simple phrases to clarify project instructions for peers (e.g., holds up a drawing of sorting bins and says, 'This is the blue bin for paper.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards or quick drawings of environmental actions (e.g., 'recycle symbol', 'tree', 'watering can'). The teacher asks, "What can we do to help the environment?" and encourages students to recall vocabulary from previous lessons. Examples: "We can recycle paper," "We can plant trees." (Vocabulary: recycle, plant, paper, trees, environment)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ and its contracted form 'I'll' /aɪl/ by saying sentences related to the upcoming project. "I will bring markers for our project." "We'll finish our posters next week." The teacher writes 'project', 'presentation day', 'due date' on the board and briefly explains their meaning with gestures and simple examples. "Our Environmental Poster project is important. The presentation day is Friday."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts related to the lesson's goal.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'presentation day' today or in the future?" (Expected answer: "In the future.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Bring a shovel,' what am I telling you to do?" (Expected answer: "To carry a shovel.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a "Project Brief" written on a large chart paper or displayed on a projector. This brief outlines the upcoming "Environmental Poster" project requirements using simple sentences, imperatives, and visual cues. It includes a title, objectives, suggested actions, and the due date. Examples: "Our Environmental Poster Project," "Objective: Help the environment," "Actions: Recycle paper, Plant trees, Save water," "Due date: Friday." (Vocabulary: project, presentation day, due date, plant, recycle, save water)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will interpret simple written instructions and suggestions for the environmental project, supported by visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this paper about?" (Expected answer: "Our environmental project / Our poster project.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads statements and students show thumbs up (True) or thumbs down (False).
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We will make a poster about animals." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We will use imperatives like 'Save water'." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice interpreting and explaining simple environmental actions using imperatives and future simple. They will also practice pronunciation of 'will' and segmenting multisyllabic words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice: "Picture-to-Phrase Mediation."</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet. Each item on the worksheet has a visual aid (e.g., a recycling symbol, a light switch turned off, a tap running water) and a blank space. Students match the visual to a pre-written imperative phrase or write a simple interpretation. Example: (Picture of recycling bin) "___ paper." (Expected answer: "Recycle paper.") (Picture of light switch off) "___ off the lights." (Expected answer: "Turn off the lights.") (Grammar: Imperatives; Vocabulary: recycle, turn off, save)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled practice: "Project Plan Prompts."</b> In pairs, students receive incomplete sentences about the environmental project. One student reads the prompt, and the other mediates by completing it using the target grammar and vocabulary. Example prompts: "Next week, we ___ (plant) trees in the garden." (Student A: "Next week, we will plant trees in the garden.") "___ (bring) recycled materials for the poster." (Student B: "Bring recycled materials for the poster.") The teacher emphasizes pronouncing 'will' and 'I'll' clearly. (Grammar: Future simple, Imperatives; Vocabulary: plant, garden, bring, materials, poster; Pronunciation: /wɪl/, /aɪl/)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction: "Explain a Poster Idea."</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair chooses one environmental action from the Project Brief (e.g., 'Save water'). One student draws a simple visual aid for that action. The other student, acting as a "facilitator," explains the visual to their partner using simple phrases and imperatives, as if explaining it for their group poster. "This picture means 'Save water.' We will put it on our poster." The partner then explains another idea. (Sociolinguistic: Discuss environmental actions; Pragmatic: Explain how to recycle/save; Vocabulary: save, water, poster)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Environmental Poster Group Facilitation."</b> Students form small groups (3-4 students) to start designing their "Environmental Poster." Each group receives a large sheet of paper and drawing materials. Within each group, students take turns acting as a "facilitator" for a segment of the discussion (e.g., deciding on the title, planning one section). The facilitator’s role is to mediate the group's work by:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writing simple prompts on a small whiteboard or sticky notes for their peers (e.g., "What will we draw here?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using visual aids (e.g., holding up a marker and asking, "Who wants to write the title?"), or pointing to a drawing and saying, "This is the blue bin for paper. We will draw it here."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarifying project instructions for peers (e.g., "Remember, we need an imperative for 'Save water.'").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encouraging participation and discussion on environmental actions using phrases like "Let's recycle plastic."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When discussing words like 'presentation,' the teacher prompts them to segment the word: 'pre-sen-ta-tion'.</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher circulates, observing and providing support, focusing on student use of future simple, imperatives, and the effective use of visuals for mediation. (Vocabulary: project, plan, write, draw, recycle, presentation; Grammar: Future simple, Imperatives; Sociolinguistic: Discuss environmental actions; Pronunciation: segmenting multisyllabic words)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment: Observation Checklist & Peer Assessment.</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to note groups' and individuals' participation, particularly their mediation skills. Students also use a simple peer checklist to assess their group members.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their poster-in-progress to the class. One student (the designated facilitator for that moment) introduces a section of the poster and uses simple phrases and visuals to explain one environmental action depicted on it, clarifying its meaning. Example: (Holding up poster) "This is our 'Recycle Paper' section. We will put paper in the blue bin."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation:</b> Used visual aids and simple phrases to clarify project instructions for peers (e.g., 'This is the blue bin for paper.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Employed imperatives for suggestions/instructions (e.g., 'Save water.') and future simple for plans (e.g., 'We will plant trees.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Included relevant vocabulary (e.g., project, environment, recycle, plant).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ and 'I'll' /aɪl/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participated actively and helped facilitate group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I helped my group understand for the poster was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One visual I will use to explain something on my poster is..."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of our Environmental Poster project makes me feel excited?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one more way I will help the environment this week?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The group facilitation roles and use of visuals were effective in promoting peer communication and understanding of the project. Students successfully used imperatives and future simple to discuss plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (the actual project presentation)? More explicit modeling of effective question-asking by facilitators might be beneficial. Ensure all groups have adequate materials and clear instructions for the final poster preparation. Reinforce the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like 'environmental' and 'presentation'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit's instructional sequence. The next "lesson" would be the actual "Environmental Poster Presentation Day," where students will present their completed posters using the communicative and mediation skills practiced. This lesson provided the foundation for their final project. </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>